#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097633} :activity/id #uuid "f97acdea-187e-5327-a13f-1e14931b5e5f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048350 :topic/id #uuid "97af7835-46ed-5be6-8581-1612329913e7" :topic/math-academy-id 1132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097855 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to know what are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola below.\n\n![](images/5d/5d3493115eb11315849d3d7c247b9f63a399c6ab7ba2c226b0b84958a47d3a21.png)\n\nThen, we proceed as follows: \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has its vertex at $\\displaystyle\\left(\\frac 32, -1\\right)$ and passes through the point $\\displaystyle\\left(1,-\\frac 34\\right).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k) = \\left(\\dfrac 32, -1\\right)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $\\displaystyle\\left(1,-\\frac 34\\right),$ we substitute $x= 1$ and $y=-\\dfrac34$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{3}{4}+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =4p(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y= 0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\dfrac12$ and $\\dfrac52.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04c720cc-0a5d-5a6c-acf5-65c378ef274b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3484 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given a Graph of the Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "47e80884-1762-5551-9161-e2231dbed002" :step/math-academy-id 19720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096504 :question/id #uuid "1456cdfb-6ae9-5439-8ebe-e5b41687dfa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5337"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403703}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "fd6bd854-7c41-5db8-95d8-5be58a99e006" :step/math-academy-id 19721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096707 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096505 :question/id #uuid "77303537-c614-5269-a22e-0bd412e7fa99" :question/math-academy-id "e-5339"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddc6c4b1-b4c4-52f8-9f25-feda3130e358" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403696}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Equation"} :step/id #uuid "003b2c9e-f558-5f16-ae29-70cb592bdea5" :step/math-academy-id 19724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096506 :question/id #uuid "4ce7b936-de41-5aeb-b980-80d9fa91314f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404016}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "82273a54-9ebb-549c-bc34-a98726936b3c" :step/math-academy-id 19725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097868 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the parabola $(x-1)^2 = \\dfrac 1 2 (y+3) ,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bbe360c9580cab435e5b737c80c5381f039d1871250cdae3c16bc686e3d0a53d.png)\n\nTo calculate the $y$-intercept of the parabola (shown as the point $A$ in the diagram), we set $x=0$ and solve for $y.$ In our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (0-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ 2 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =2-3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-1).$\n\nOn the other hand, to find the $x$-intercepts (shown as the points $B$ and $C$ in the diagram), we set $y=0$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(1-\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right)$ and the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1+\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}},0\\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9511803-ede3-5b03-b34f-87d1bb050901" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3eccc6e2-615b-5126-b1a2-9f9bb514c40d" :step/math-academy-id 23091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097573}}] :activity/title "Calculating Intercepts of Parabolas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096904}]}
